# Swift Package Manager
#
# Add this line if you want to avoid checking in source code from Swift Package Manager dependencies.
Packages/
Package.pins
# Package.resolved is deliberately NOT ignored — both copies of it. The stock
# template ignores it because it is written for libraries, which should resolve
# against whatever a consumer already has. This is an app: it wants every build
# — a new machine, a release, CI — to resolve the same dependency versions.
#
# There are two because Xcode and SwiftPM read different ones. `swift build`
# reads the package's own Package.resolved next to Package.swift; Xcode builds
# this through Project.xcworkspace and reads
# Project.xcworkspace/xcshareddata/swiftpm/Package.resolved. Only the second
# was missing, which is how a machine could resolve client-sdk-swift 2.17.0
# while the committed lockfile said 2.14.1 — and 2.17.0 vendors a nanopb that
# collides with Firebase's. Keep the two in step; Package.swift caps LiveKit so
# that drifting cannot reach 2.17.0 either way.
#*.xcodeproj

Carthage/Build/

